\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we propose a novel method to learn a shared representation for multiple tasks. Our method is based on the idea that the shared representation should be able to capture the commonalities among different tasks while being discriminative enough to perform well on each task individually. To achieve this, we propose a novel objective function that encourages the shared representation to be close to the representations learned by a single-task model trained on each task, while also encouraging the shared representation to be discriminative by maximizing the mutual information between the shared representation and the task label. We show that our method can achieve state-of-the-art performance on several multi-task learning benchmarks, and even outperform single-task models trained on each task individually.

